      "text": "To solve for \\( a_{2009} \\) in the sequence defined by \\( a_n = a_{n-1} a_{n+1} \\) for all \\( n \\ge 2 \\), and given \\( a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7} \\) and \\( a_{1776} = 13 + \\sqrt{7} \\), we can proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Analyze the recurrence relation:**\n    \\[\n    a_n = a_{n-1} a_{n+1} \\implies \\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n-1}} = \\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}\n    \\]\n    This shows that the sequence is a geometric sequence.\n\n2. **Determine the common ratio \\( r \\):**\n    Since \\( a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7} \\) and \\( a_{1776} = 13 + \\sqrt{7} \\), we can use the properties of geometric sequences to find \\( r \\). The general form of a geometric sequence is \\( a_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\).\n\n    Given:\n    \\[\n    a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7}, \\quad a_{1776} = 13 + \\sqrt{7}\n    \\]\n\n    We need to find \\( r \\). Since \\( 1775 = 13 \\cdot 140 + 5 \\), we can write:\n    \\[\n    a_{1776} = a_1 \\cdot r^{1775}\n    \\]\n    Thus,\n    \\[\n    13 + \\sqrt{7} = (1 + \\sqrt{7}) \\cdot r^{1775}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\( r^{1775} \\):**\n    To find \\( r^{1775} \\), we can use the relationship:\n    \\[\n    r^{1775} = \\frac{13 + \\sqrt{7}}{1 + \\sqrt{7}}\n    \\]\n\n    Simplify the right-hand side:\n    \\[\n    r^{1775} = \\frac{13 + \\sqrt{7}}{1 + \\sqrt{7}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{7}}{1 - \\sqrt{7}} = \\frac{(13 + \\sqrt{7})(1 - \\sqrt{7})}{(1 + \\sqrt{7})(1 - \\sqrt{7})} = \\frac{13 - 13\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{7} - 7}{1 - 7} = \\frac{6 - 12\\sqrt{7}}{-6} = \\sqrt{7} - 1\n    \\]\n\n4. **Find \\( a_{2009} \\):**\n    Since \\( a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7} \\) and \\( r = \\sqrt{7} - 1 \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    a_{2009} = a_1 \\cdot r^{2008}\n    \\]\n\n    We know \\( r^{2008} \\) can be expressed as:\n    \\[\n    r^{2008} = (r^{1775})^{\\frac{2008}{1775}} = (\\sqrt{7} - 1)^{\\frac{2008}{1775}}\n    \\]\n\n    Simplify \\( \\frac{2008}{1775} \\):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{2008}{1775} = 1 + \\frac{233}{1775}\n    \\]\n\n    Hence:\n    \\[\n    r^{2008} = (\\sqrt{7